# HOOD — this realm's holdings, mapped

The map the session digest prints whole at startup, so the session opens knowing the
lay of the land. It is a **map, not a store**: it names where things live and points at
the door into each shelf. A path here that stops existing is a bug in this file — fix
the map, do not silently drop the line.

This file is a **stub**, copied once to `$YMIR_HOME/svartalfaheim/<realm>/HOOD.md`,
and never overwritten.

```
seat  svartalfaheim/<realm>/          the realm seat (this file, the realm env, projects)
hoard $YMIR_HOME                      the private home — repo-$YMIR_HOME_REPO, private
tree  <the checkout>                  the public repository
```

## The hoard — `$YMIR_HOME/hodd/`

```
hodd/
├── data/              the operator's working record
│   ├── operator.md    standing preferences — read at every session start
│   ├── learnings.md   curated, dated operational facts
│   ├── machines.md    this operator's own computers
│   ├── projects.md    the registry, in prose
│   └── realm.md       pins the active realm
├── identity/          who and what the house is
│   ├── companies.yaml   the houses the operator IS
│   ├── clients.yaml     the companies a house SERVES
│   ├── projects.yaml    the project registry, with its git block
│   ├── workspaces.yaml  the realms a project may name
│   └── seats.yaml       which figure stands on which machine
├── docs/              the curated knowledge shelf — ONE door: docs/INDEX.md
├── memory/            the well · the daily log · the chained audit ledger
├── plans/             live personal working plans
├── secrets/           by reference only, never read by hand
└── reference/         retired documents — moved here, never deleted
```

## Where an output goes

Business and strategy · marketing and social · software specs · personal schedules ·
daily logs · the global audit ledger — each has its own shelf, and **none of them is
the public repository**. Private data lives in the home and nowhere else.

## What is deliberately not here

- **No secret values.** This file names shelves; secrets are read through the vault door.
- **No other realm's business.** A neighbouring realm is named, never read.
- **No host addresses.** A cross-host path is a tailnet name or a tunnel, resolved at
  run time — never a LAN address.

